{"ts": "2026-06-26T11:59:53+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "agent_framework", "trace_id": "aaaae333fad37014fa11cd83f86e4a1e", "span_id": "7a9c7fca733aba1f", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "02E8C970", "msg": "{'role': 'assistant', 'parts': [{'type': 'tool_call', 'id': 'fc_8b80ac55-3852-4c1a-af0e-9b7714a8c215', 'name': 'query_knowledge_base', 'arguments': '{\"question\":\"When should senior management be involved regarding model risk?\"}'}]}"}
{"ts": "2026-06-26T11:59:53+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "agent_framework", "trace_id": "aaaae333fad37014fa11cd83f86e4a1e", "span_id": "7a9c7fca733aba1f", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "02E8C970", "msg": "{'role': 'tool', 'parts': [{'type': 'tool_call_response', 'id': 'fc_8b80ac55-3852-4c1a-af0e-9b7714a8c215', 'response': 'When should senior management be involved regarding model risk?'}]}"}
{"ts": "2026-06-26T11:59:53+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "agent_framework", "trace_id": "aaaae333fad37014fa11cd83f86e4a1e", "span_id": "7a9c7fca733aba1f", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "02E8C970", "msg": "{'role': 'assistant', 'parts': [{'type': 'tool_call', 'id': 'fc_bb7cd70b-59ef-4b58-b061-645cc4b4d979', 'name': 'query_knowledge_base', 'arguments': '{\"question\":\"FAB policy senior management involvement model risk thresholds\"}'}]}"}
{"ts": "2026-06-26T11:59:53+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "agent_framework", "trace_id": "aaaae333fad37014fa11cd83f86e4a1e", "span_id": "7a9c7fca733aba1f", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "02E8C970", "msg": "{'role': 'tool', 'parts': [{'type': 'tool_call_response', 'id': 'fc_bb7cd70b-59ef-4b58-b061-645cc4b4d979', 'response': \"FAB's senior management involvement in model risk is governed by the **FAB Model Risk Management Framework v3.1**, which aligns with the mandatory standards set in **CBUAE Circular No. 2024/BSE/047** (AI Governance). \\n\\nInvolvement thresholds and accountability structures are triggered by specific model risk classifications and performance anomalies:\\n\\n### 1. Senior Management Accountability Roles\\n*   **AI Risk Officer:** A designated member of senior management—no lower than the level of the Chief Risk Officer or equivalent—must serve as the AI Risk Officer. This individual is responsible for overseeing the implementation of AI and model risk governance across the bank. [CBUAE Circular 2024/BSE/047 · Art 2.1.2]\\n*   **Board Oversight:** The Board of Directors must adopt and approve a written AI Governance Policy at least annually, which must address the bank's risk appetite and accountability structures for AI and model development. [CBUAE Circular 2024/BSE/047 · Art 2.1.1]\\n\\n### 2. Model Risk Classification Thresholds\\nModels are classified into three tiers. **Tier 1 (High Risk)** models require the strictest senior management oversight, mandatory human-in-the-loop controls, and full independent validation before deployment.\\n\\n**Tier 1 (High Risk) Threshold Criteria [FAB MRM Framework v3.1 · Sec 2]:**\\n*   **Financial Impact:** Direct Profit & Loss (P&L) impact or credit decision influence above **AED 10 million**.\\n*   **Regulatory Impact:** Models used to calculate regulatory capital.\\n*   **Credit Pricing:** All credit pricing agents, including Large Language Model (LLM)-based agents used to propose credit facility pricing.\\n\\n### 3. Escalation and Monitoring Thresholds for Senior Management\\nSenior management (specifically the AI Risk Officer) must be notified when model performance shows signs of degradation. The primary threshold for this escalation is based on human override rates:\\n\\n*   **Systematic Override Threshold:** If an AI or model recommendation is overridden by human reviewers at a rate exceeding **25% over a rolling 90-day period**, it must be reported to the AI Risk Officer for review and investigation into potential model performance degradation. [CBUAE Circular 2024/BSE/047 · Art 5.2.2]\"}]}"}
{"ts": "2026-06-26T11:59:56+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "agent_framework", "trace_id": "aaaae333fad37014fa11cd83f86e4a1e", "span_id": "7a9c7fca733aba1f", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "02E8C970", "msg": "{'role': 'assistant', 'parts': [{'type': 'text', 'content': '**When senior management must be involved in model risk at FAB**\\n\\n- **Accountability role** – An AI\\u202fRisk Officer, who must be a senior‑management member (at least Chief Risk Officer level), is required to oversee AI and model‑risk governance.  \\n  *Source: FAB Model Risk Management Framework v3.1 – Sec\\u202f2; CBUAE Circular\\u202f2024/BSE/047 – Art\\u202f2.1.2*\\n\\n- **Board oversight** – The Board of Directors must approve a written AI Governance Policy annually, covering risk‑appetite and accountability for AI/model development.  \\n  *Source: CBUAE Circular\\u202f2024/BSE/047 – Art\\u202f2.1.1*\\n\\n- **Model‑risk classification that triggers senior‑management oversight**  \\n  *Tier\\u202f1 (High‑risk) models* require senior‑management review, mandatory human‑in‑the‑loop controls, and full independent validation before deployment.  \\n  Criteria for Tier\\u202f1:  \\n  • Direct P&L impact or credit‑decision influence **>\\u202fAED\\u202f10\\u202fmillion**  \\n  • Models used for regulatory‑capital calculations  \\n  • All credit‑pricing agents, including LLM‑based pricing models  \\n  *Source: FAB Model Risk Management Framework v3.1 – Sec\\u202f2*\\n\\n- **Escalation threshold for ongoing monitoring** – Senior management (AI\\u202fRisk Officer) must be notified when model recommendations are overridden by humans at a rate **exceeding 25\\u202f% over a rolling 90‑day period**. This signals possible model‑performance degradation and requires senior‑management review.  \\n  *Source: CBUAE Circular\\u202f2024/BSE/047 – Art\\u202f5.2.2*'}]}"}
